docs/guide/color-grading.md:34:The order the grade is applied in is fixed: Color first, then Light, Wheels, Curves (tone curves, then the hue curves) and the Look. The clip's filters from the Picture tab's **Filters** section split around the grade: Pixelate, Blur and Sharpen act on the picture before it, and Sepia, Noir, Vignette and Invert act after the Look, in the order of their slots. That matters when you combine a filter such as Noir or Vignette with a grade. A camera-log conversion set on the media comes before all of them (see [HDR and colour management](hdr-and-color.md)).
docs/guide/color-grading.md:204:There is no Picture tab for an adjustment clip, because it has nothing to place or crop. Point filters (Sepia, Noir, Vignette, Invert) given to it by Paste Attributes or by an agent also apply to the picture behind it.
docs/guide/color-grading.md:213:*Agents:* `add_adjustment_clip` (`track`, `at`, `duration`, which defaults to 5 s). Then use `set_video` with `color`, `wheels`, `curves`, `look`, `filters`, `qualifier` and `opacity`, which sets the strength.
docs/guide/color-grading.md:275:Some things are not part of the grade, so Show Ungraded leaves them on: a camera-log conversion on the media, filters, the chroma key, and placement. Exports, **Save Frame…** and the look gallery's thumbnails never see the switch.
docs/guide/color-grading.md:320:Copy a graded clip (⌘C), select other clips, and choose **Edit ▸ Paste Attributes** (⌥⌘V). The copied clip's video settings replace each selected clip's in one undo step: its grade, look, filters and the rest of its picture settings, including position and scale. Where the copied clip has sound, its audio settings come too. A clip's place, speed, actions, transitions, name and label are never copied.
